<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Experiment Setup</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Prompt Formats</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ A.1 Prompt Formats ‣ Appendix A Experiment Setup ‣ Cross-Layer Attention Probing for Fine-Grained Hallucination Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuhn et al. (2023)</a></cite> and figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ A.1 Prompt Formats ‣ Appendix A Experiment Setup ‣ Cross-Layer Attention Probing for Fine-Grained Hallucination Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite> show the prompt formats used for generating LLM responses for the three tasks considered in the main experiments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09700v1/prompt_format1.png" id="A1.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/22;" width="191" height="22" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="A1.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt Format: Natural Questions, Trivia QA</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09700v1/prompt_format2.png" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/97;" width="262" height="97" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A1.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt Format: Strategy QA</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Data Labelling and Dataset Statistics</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Trivia QA and Natural Questions, each LLM response is labelled as hallucinated/non-hallucinated using a rouge-1 cut-off of 0.3, following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuhn et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al. (2023)</a></cite>, where the rouge labels are validated against human annotated labels finding a 0.96 accuracy. For StrategyQA, each LLM response is labelled by matching the final answer produced after the COT against the gold reference of YES/NO, following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ A.2 Data Labelling and Dataset Statistics ‣ Appendix A Experiment Setup ‣ Cross-Layer Attention Probing for Fine-Grained Hallucination Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the number of prompts used, number of additional responses sampled per prompt at high temperature and the hallucination rate among greedy and sampled responses. We note that since we use LLMs of at most 8B parameters, given the wide range of facts queried and with no access to external information, such high hallucination rates are expected. For NQ with Llama3.1-I-8B, we find a very high hallucination rate of ¿95% among greedy responses and exclude this from the analysis. We note that high hallucination rates for NQ are in line with observations in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023)</a></cite> and is generally attributed to the difference between typical LLM pre-training data and data used for creating NQ (Google search queries).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="A1.T6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Statistics of the datasets used. Shows number of prompts, additional responses sampled per prompt and % of hallucinated responses in the train and test splits.</span></figcaption>
